The program itself is small — read four integers, stuff them into a bit-field struct, print them — so the translation work was almost entirely about the two places where C semantics are not obvious:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Bit-field truncation. [38;5;10munsigned int x : 2[0m and [38;5;10my : 3[0m silently discard high bits on assignment. Rust has no bit-fields, so [38;5;10mFoo::new[0m masks with [38;5;10m& 0x3[0m and [38;5;10m& 0x7[0m. This is real observable behavior, not a corner case: input [38;5;10m7 15[0m prints [38;5;10m3 7[0m. [38;5;10mbool b : 1[0m combined with the [38;5;10m!!b[0m at the call site just yields 0 or 1.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
glibc [38;5;10mscanf[0m behavior. [38;5;10mmain[0m ignores every return value, so what matters is the side effects. A [38;5;10mScanner[0m over stdin reproduces them: leading whitespace is skipped across newlines, a match failure leaves the destination variable at its initializer ([38;5;10m0[0m) and pushes back only one byte — so a consumed sign character stays consumed and one bad token poisons all subsequent reads. Overflow follows glibc's [38;5;10mstrtol[0m/[38;5;10mstrtoul[0m path: clamp to the 64-bit limit, then truncate into the 32-bit destination. That is why [38;5;10m-1[0m for [38;5;10m%u[0m prints [38;5;10m3[0m after masking, and why an absurdly large [38;5;10m%d[0m value prints [38;5;10m-1[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two notes on judgment calls. I reproduced the C's habit of ignoring [38;5;10mscanf[0m return codes rather than reporting malformed input, per the no-bug-fixing requirement. And glibc's overflow clamping is technically undefined behavior in C, so the "correct" output for those inputs is really "whatever glibc does" — I matched the local glibc, which is what the differential tests confirm. A different libc could differ there.[0m[0m
